#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#, no-wrap
msgid "Using Qiskit to implement ideas from the single-systems lesson."
msgstr "Paggamit ng Qiskit para ipatupad ang mga ideya mula sa aralin tungkol sa single systems."

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#, no-wrap
msgid "Qiskit implementation"
msgstr "Implementasyon sa Qiskit"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<OpenInLabBanner notebookPath=\"learning/courses/basics-of-quantum-information/single-systems/qiskit-implementation.ipynb\" />\n"
msgstr "<OpenInLabBanner notebookPath=\"learning/courses/basics-of-quantum-information/single-systems/qiskit-implementation.ipynb\" />\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In this section, we'll take a look at some Qiskit implementations of the concepts introduced in this lesson.\n"
"If you wish to run these implementations yourself, which is strongly encouraged, consult the [Install Qiskit](/guides/install-qiskit) page in the\n"
"[IBM Quantum Documentation](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com) for details on how to set up Qiskit.\n"
msgstr ""
"Sa seksyong ito, titingnan natin ang ilang implementasyon sa Qiskit ng mga konsepto na ipinakilala sa araling ito.\n"
"Kung gusto mong patakbuhin ang mga implementasyong ito nang ikaw mismo, na lubos na hinihikayat, tingnan ang pahina ng [Install Qiskit](/guides/install-qiskit) sa\n"
"[IBM Quantum Documentation](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com) para sa mga detalye kung paano i-set up ang Qiskit.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"It should be understood that Qiskit is under continual development, and is principally focused on maximizing the performance of the quantum computers it is used to operate, which themselves continue to evolve.\n"
"As a result, Qiskit is subject to changes that may occasionally lead to code deprecation.\n"
"With this in mind, we'll always execute the following commands prior to presenting examples of Qiskit code in this course, so that it is clear which version of Qiskit has been used.\n"
"Starting with Qiskit v1.0, this is a simple way to see what version of Qiskit is currently installed.\n"
msgstr ""
"Dapat maintindihan na ang Qiskit ay patuloy na nino-develop, at pangunahing nakatuon sa pag-maximize ng performance ng mga quantum computer na ginagamit nito, na patuloy ding nagbabago.\n"
"Dahil dito, ang Qiskit ay sumasailalim sa mga pagbabago na maaaring paminsan-minsan ay magdulot ng code deprecation.\n"
"Isinasaalang-alang ito, palagi nating isasagawa ang mga sumusunod na command bago ipakita ang mga halimbawa ng Qiskit code sa kursong ito, para malinaw kung aling bersyon ng Qiskit ang ginamit.\n"
"Simula sa Qiskit v1.0, ito ay isang simpleng paraan para makita kung anong bersyon ng Qiskit ang kasalukuyang naka-install.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "If you are running this in a cloud-based Python environment, you might need to install some of the following packages:\n"
msgstr "Kung pinapatakbo mo ito sa isang cloud-based na Python environment, maaaring kailangan mong i-install ang ilan sa mga sumusunod na package:\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Vectors and matrices in Python {#vectors-and-matrices-in-python}"
msgstr "Mga vector at matrix sa Python {#vectors-and-matrices-in-python}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Qiskit uses the Python programming language, so before discussing Qiskit specifically, it may be helpful to very briefly discuss matrix and vector computations in Python.\n"
msgstr "Gumagamit ang Qiskit ng programming language na Python, kaya bago talakayin ang Qiskit nang espesipiko, maaaring makatulong na maikli munang talakayin ang matrix at vector computations sa Python.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In Python, matrix and vector computations can be performed using the `array` class from the `NumPy` library, which provides functionality for many numerical and scientific computations.\n"
"The following code loads this library, defines two column vectors, `ket0` and `ket1`, corresponding to the qubit state vectors $\\vert 0\\rangle$ and $\\vert 1\\rangle$, and then prints their average.\n"
msgstr ""
"Sa Python, ang matrix at vector computations ay maaaring isagawa gamit ang `array` class mula sa `NumPy` library, na nagbibigay ng functionality para sa maraming numerical at scientific computations.\n"
"Ang sumusunod na code ay naglo-load ng library na ito, nagtatakda ng dalawang column vector, `ket0` at `ket1`, na tumutugma sa qubit state vector na $\\vert 0\\rangle$ at $\\vert 1\\rangle$, at pagkatapos ay ini-print ang kanilang average.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We can also use `array` to create matrices that can represent operations.\n"
msgstr "Maaari rin tayong gumamit ng `array` para gumawa ng mga matrix na maaaring kumatawan sa mga operasyon.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Please note that all code appearing within a given lesson in this course is expected to be run sequentially.\n"
"Therefore, we don't need to import `NumPy` again here, because it has already been imported.\n"
msgstr ""
"Pakitandaan na ang lahat ng code na lumalabas sa loob ng isang aralin sa kursong ito ay inaasahang tatakbuhin nang sunud-sunod.\n"
"Kaya naman, hindi na natin kailangang i-import muli ang `NumPy` dito, dahil na-import na ito.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Matrix multiplication, including matrix-vector multiplication as a special case, can be performed using the `@` operator.\n"
msgstr "Ang matrix multiplication, kasama na ang matrix-vector multiplication bilang isang espesyal na kaso, ay maaaring isagawa gamit ang `@` operator.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This output formatting leaves something to be desired, visually speaking.\n"
"One solution, for situations that demand something prettier, is to use the`array_to_latex` function in Qiskit, from the `qiskit.visualization` module.\n"
"Note that, in the code that follows, we're using Python's generic `display` function (especially in notebooks), because it can render objects using richer representations, whereas `print()` simply uses the object's string representation. This is similar to how `NumPy` arrays provide their own specialized formatting while Python lists use the default list representation.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang output formatting na ito ay may kung ano mang kulang, sa visual na paraan.\n"
"Isang solusyon, para sa mga sitwasyong nangangailangan ng mas maganda, ay ang gamitin ang`array_to_latex` function sa Qiskit, mula sa `qiskit.visualization` module.\n"
"Tandaan na sa code na susunod, gumagamit tayo ng generic na `display` function ng Python (lalo na sa mga notebook), dahil kaya nitong i-render ang mga object gamit ang mas mayamang representasyon, samantalang ang `print()` ay simpleng gumagamit ng string representation ng object. Katulad ito ng kung paano nagbibigay ang mga `NumPy` array ng kanilang sariling specialized na formatting habang ginagamit ng mga Python list ang default na list representation.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$$\n"
msgstr "$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"1  \\\\\n"
" 0  \\\\\n"
" \\end{bmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"\\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"1  \\\\\n"
" 0  \\\\\n"
" \\end{bmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"1 & 1  \\\\\n"
" 0 & 0  \\\\\n"
" \\end{bmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"\\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"1 & 1  \\\\\n"
" 0 & 0  \\\\\n"
" \\end{bmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"1 & \\frac{3}{4}  \\\\\n"
" 0 & \\frac{1}{4}  \\\\\n"
" \\end{bmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"\\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"1 & \\frac{3}{4}  \\\\\n"
" 0 & \\frac{1}{4}  \\\\\n"
" \\end{bmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "States, measurements, and operations {#states-measurements-and-operations}"
msgstr "Mga state, measurement, at operasyon {#states-measurements-and-operations}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Qiskit includes several classes that allow for states, measurements, and operations to be created and manipulated — so there is no need to program everything required to simulate quantum states, measurements, and operations in Python.\n"
"Some examples to help you to get started are included below.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang Qiskit ay naglalaman ng ilang mga class na nagpapahintulot sa mga state, measurement, at operasyon na gawin at manipulahin — kaya naman hindi na kailangang i-program ang lahat ng kailangan para i-simulate ang mga quantum state, measurement, at operasyon sa Python.\n"
"Kasama sa ibaba ang ilang halimbawa para makatulong sa iyo na magsimula.\n"

#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Define and display state vectors {#define-and-display-state-vectors}"
msgstr "Tukuyin at ipakita ang mga state vector {#define-and-display-state-vectors}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The`Statevector` class in Qiskit provides functionality for defining and manipulating quantum state vectors.\n"
"In the code that follows, the `Statevector` class is imported and a few vectors are defined.\n"
"(We're also importing the `sqrt` function from the `NumPy` library to compute a square root.\n"
"This function could, alternatively, be called as `np.sqrt` provided that `NumPy` has already been imported, as it has above; this is merely a different way to import and use this specific function alone.)\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang`Statevector` class sa Qiskit ay nagbibigay ng functionality para sa pagtukoy at pag-manipulate ng mga quantum state vector.\n"
"Sa code na susunod, ang `Statevector` class ay ini-import at ilang vector ang tinukoy.\n"
"(Ino-import din natin ang `sqrt` function mula sa `NumPy` library para kalkulahin ang square root.\n"
"Ang function na ito ay maaaring, bilang alternatibo, tawaging `np.sqrt` basta na-import na ang `NumPy`, tulad ng ginawa sa itaas; ito ay isang ibang paraan lamang para i-import at gamitin ang partikular na function na ito nang mag-isa.)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The `Statevector` class includes a `draw` method for displaying state vectors in a variety of ways, including\n"
"`text` for plain text, `latex` for rendered LaTeX, and `latex_source` for LaTeX code, which can be handy for cutting and pasting into documents.\n"
"(Use `print` rather than `display` to show LaTeX code for best results.)\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang `Statevector` class ay naglalaman ng `draw` method para ipakita ang mga state vector sa iba't ibang paraan, kasama ang\n"
"`text` para sa plain text, `latex` para sa rendered LaTeX, at `latex_source` para sa LaTeX code, na maaaring maging kapaki-pakinabang para sa pag-cut at pag-paste sa mga dokumento.\n"
"(Gamitin ang `print` sa halip na `display` para ipakita ang LaTeX code para sa pinakamainam na resulta.)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$$\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} |0\\rangle+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} |1\\rangle$$\n"
msgstr "$$\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} |0\\rangle+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} |1\\rangle$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The `Statevector` class also includes the `is_valid` method, which checks to see if a given vector is a valid quantum state vector (in other words, that it has Euclidean norm equal to 1):\n"
msgstr "Ang `Statevector` class ay naglalaman din ng `is_valid` method, na sinusuri kung ang isang ibinigay na vector ay isang valid na quantum state vector (sa ibang salita, na mayroon itong Euclidean norm na katumbas ng 1):\n"

#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Simulating measurements using `Statevector` {#simulating-measurements-using-statevector}"
msgstr "Pag-simulate ng mga measurement gamit ang `Statevector` {#simulating-measurements-using-statevector}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Next we will see one way that measurements of quantum states can be simulated in Qiskit, using the `measure` method from the `Statevector` class.\n"
"Let's use the same qubit state vector `v` defined previously.\n"
msgstr ""
"Susunod ay makikita natin ang isang paraan na ang mga measurement ng quantum state ay maaaring i-simulate sa Qiskit, gamit ang `measure` method mula sa `Statevector` class.\n"
"Gamitin natin ang parehong qubit state vector `v` na tinukoy kanina.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$$(\\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{2 i}{3}) |0\\rangle- \\frac{2}{3} |1\\rangle$$\n"
msgstr "$$(\\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{2 i}{3}) |0\\rangle- \\frac{2}{3} |1\\rangle$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Running the `measure` method simulates a standard basis measurement.\n"
"It returns the outcome of that measurement, plus the new quantum state vector of the system after the measurement.\n"
"(Here we're using Python's `print` function with an `f` prefix for formatted printing with embedded expressions.)\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang pagpapatakbo ng `measure` method ay nag-si-simulate ng isang standard basis measurement.\n"
"Ibinabalik nito ang resulta ng measurement na iyon, kasama ang bagong quantum state vector ng sistema pagkatapos ng measurement.\n"
"(Dito ay gumagamit tayo ng `print` function ng Python na may `f` prefix para sa formatted printing na may embedded na mga expression.)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$$- |1\\rangle$$\n"
msgstr "$$- |1\\rangle$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Measurement outcomes are probabilistic, so this method can return different results when run multiple times.\n"
"For the particular example of the vector `v` defined above, the `measure` method defines the quantum state vector after the measurement takes place to be\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang mga resulta ng measurement ay probabilistiko, kaya ang method na ito ay maaaring magbalik ng iba't ibang resulta kapag pinatakbo nang maraming beses.\n"
"Para sa partikular na halimbawa ng vector `v` na tinukoy sa itaas, tinutukoy ng `measure` method ang quantum state vector pagkatapos ng measurement na magiging\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\biggl(\\frac{1 + 2i}{\\sqrt{5}}\\biggr) \\vert 0\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\biggl(\\frac{1 + 2i}{\\sqrt{5}}\\biggr) \\vert 0\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(rather than $\\vert 0\\rangle$) or\n"
msgstr "(sa halip na $\\vert 0\\rangle$) o\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"- \\vert 1\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"- \\vert 1\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(rather than $\\vert 1\\rangle$), depending on the measurement outcome.\n"
"In both cases, the alternatives to $\\vert 0\\rangle$ and $\\vert 1\\rangle$ are, in fact, equivalent to these state vectors; they are said to to *equivalent up to a global phase* because one is equal to the other multiplied by a complex number on the unit circle.\n"
"This issue is discussed in greater detail in the [Quantum circuits](/learning/courses/basics-of-quantum-information/quantum-circuits/introduction) lesson, and can safely be ignored for now.\n"
msgstr ""
"(sa halip na $\\vert 1\\rangle$), depende sa resulta ng measurement.\n"
"Sa parehong kaso, ang mga alternatibo sa $\\vert 0\\rangle$ at $\\vert 1\\rangle$ ay, sa katunayan, katumbas ng mga state vector na ito; sinasabing sila ay *katumbas hanggang sa isang global phase* dahil ang isa ay katumbas ng isa pa na pinarami ng isang complex number sa unit circle.\n"
"Ang isyung ito ay tinalakay nang mas detalyado sa aralin ng [Quantum circuits](/learning/courses/basics-of-quantum-information/quantum-circuits/introduction), at ligtas na maaaring balewalain sa ngayon.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "`Statevector` will throw an error if the `measure` method is applied to an invalid quantum state vector.\n"
msgstr "Ang `Statevector` ay magta-throw ng error kung ang `measure` method ay inilapat sa isang invalid na quantum state vector.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`Statevector` also comes with a `sample_counts` method that allows for the simulation of any number of measurements on the system, each time starting with a fresh copy of the state. For example, the following code shows the outcome of measuring the vector `v` $1000$ times, which (with high probability) results in the outcome $0$ approximately $5$ out of every $9$ times (or about $556$ of the $1000$ trials) and the outcome $1$ approximately $4$ out of every $9$ times (or about $444$ out of the $1000$ trials).\n"
"The code that follows also demonstrates the `plot_histogram` function from the `qiskit.visualization` module for visualizing the results.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang `Statevector` ay may kasamang `sample_counts` method din na nagpapahintulot sa pag-simulate ng kahit ilang measurement sa sistema, sa bawat oras na nagsisimula sa isang sariwang kopya ng state. Halimbawa, ang sumusunod na code ay nagpapakita ng resulta ng pagsukat ng vector `v` $1000$ na beses, na (na may mataas na posibilidad) ay nagbubunga ng resulta na $0$ humigit-kumulang $5$ sa bawat $9$ beses (o humigit-kumulang $556$ sa $1000$ na pagsubok) at ang resulta na $1$ humigit-kumulang $4$ sa bawat $9$ beses (o humigit-kumulang $444$ sa $1000$ na pagsubok).\n"
"Ipinapakita rin ng code na susunod ang `plot_histogram` function mula sa `qiskit.visualization` module para visualisahin ang mga resulta.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/basics-of-quantum-information/single-systems/qiskit-implementation/extracted-outputs/fde6ccf9-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/basics-of-quantum-information/single-systems/qiskit-implementation/extracted-outputs/fde6ccf9-0.avif)\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Running this code on your own multiple times, with different numbers of samples in place of $1000$, may be helpful for developing some intuition for how the number of trials influences the number of times each outcome appears.\n"
"With more and more samples, the fraction of samples for each possibility is likely to get closer and closer to the corresponding probability.\n"
"This phenomenon, more generally speaking, is known as the *law of large numbers* in probability theory.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang pagpapatakbo ng code na ito nang maraming beses, na may iba't ibang bilang ng sample sa halip na $1000$, ay maaaring makatulong sa pagbuo ng ilang intuisyon kung paano naiimpluwensyahan ng bilang ng mga pagsubok ang bilang ng mga beses na lumalabas ang bawat resulta.\n"
"Sa mas marami at mas maraming sample, ang fraction ng mga sample para sa bawat posibilidad ay malamang na magiging mas malapit at mas malapit sa katumbas na posibilidad.\n"
"Ang fenomenong ito, sa mas pangkalahatang paraan, ay kilala bilang *batas ng malalaking numero* sa probability theory.\n"

#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Perform operations with `Operator` and `Statevector` {#perform-operations-with-operator-and-statevector}"
msgstr "Magsagawa ng mga operasyon gamit ang `Operator` at `Statevector` {#perform-operations-with-operator-and-statevector}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Unitary operations can be defined in Qiskit using the `Operator` class, as in the example that follows.\n"
"This class includes a `draw` method with similar arguments to `Statevector`.\n"
"Note that the `latex` option produces results equivalent to `array_to_latex`.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang mga unitary operation ay maaaring tukuyin sa Qiskit gamit ang `Operator` class, tulad ng sa halimbawa na sumusunod.\n"
"Ang class na ito ay naglalaman ng `draw` method na may katulad na mga argumento sa `Statevector`.\n"
"Tandaan na ang `latex` option ay nagpo-produce ng mga resulta na katumbas ng `array_to_latex`.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"1 & 0  \\\\\n"
" 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2} i}{2}  \\\\\n"
" \\end{bmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"\\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"1 & 0  \\\\\n"
" 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2} i}{2}  \\\\\n"
" \\end{bmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We can apply a unitary operation to a state vector using the `evolve` method.\n"
msgstr "Maaari nating ilapat ang isang unitary operation sa isang state vector gamit ang `evolve` method.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$$(0.1464466094 - 0.3535533906 i) |0\\rangle+(-0.3535533906 + 0.8535533906 i) |1\\rangle$$\n"
msgstr "$$(0.1464466094 - 0.3535533906 i) |0\\rangle+(-0.3535533906 + 0.8535533906 i) |1\\rangle$$\n"

#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "A preview of quantum circuits {#a-preview-of-quantum-circuits}"
msgstr "Isang preview ng quantum circuits {#a-preview-of-quantum-circuits}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Quantum circuits won't be formally introduced until the [Quantum circuits](/learning/courses/basics-of-quantum-information/quantum-circuits/introduction) lesson, which is the third lesson in this course, but we can nevertheless experiment with composing qubit unitary operations using the`QuantumCircuit` class in Qiskit.\n"
"In particular, we may define a quantum circuit (which, in this case, will simply be a sequence of unitary operations performed on a single qubit) as follows.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang mga quantum circuit ay hindi pormal na ipapakilala hanggang sa aralin ng [Quantum circuits](/learning/courses/basics-of-quantum-information/quantum-circuits/introduction), na siyang ikatlong aralin sa kursong ito, ngunit maaari pa rin tayong mag-eksperimento sa pag-compose ng mga qubit unitary operation gamit ang`QuantumCircuit` class sa Qiskit.\n"
"Sa partikular, maaari tayong tumukoy ng quantum circuit (na, sa kasong ito, ay magiging isang pagkakasunud-sunod ng mga unitary operation na isinasagawa sa isang qubit) tulad ng sumusunod.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/basics-of-quantum-information/single-systems/qiskit-implementation/extracted-outputs/68490946-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/basics-of-quantum-information/single-systems/qiskit-implementation/extracted-outputs/68490946-0.avif)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here we're using the `draw` method from the `QuantumCircuit` class with the `mpl` renderer (short for `Matplotlib`, a Python visualization library).\n"
"This is the only renderer we'll use for quantum circuits in this course, but there are other options, including a text-based and a LaTeX-based renderer.\n"
msgstr ""
"Dito ay ginagamit natin ang `draw` method mula sa `QuantumCircuit` class na may `mpl` renderer (maikli para sa `Matplotlib`, isang Python visualization library).\n"
"Ito ang tanging renderer na gagamitin natin para sa mga quantum circuit sa kursong ito, ngunit may iba pang mga opsyon, kasama ang isang text-based at isang LaTeX-based na renderer.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The operations are applied sequentially, starting on the left and ending on the right in the diagram.\n"
"A handy way to get the unitary matrix corresponding to this circuit is to use the `from_circuit` method from the `Operator` class.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang mga operasyon ay inilalapat nang sunud-sunod, nagsisimula sa kaliwa at nagtatapos sa kanan sa diagram.\n"
"Isang maginhawang paraan para makuha ang unitary matrix na tumutugma sa circuit na ito ay ang gamitin ang `from_circuit` method mula sa `Operator` class.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"0.1464466094 - 0.3535533906 i & 0.8535533906 + 0.3535533906 i  \\\\\n"
" -0.3535533906 + 0.8535533906 i & 0.3535533906 + 0.1464466094 i  \\\\\n"
" \\end{bmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"\\begin{bmatrix}\n"
"0.1464466094 - 0.3535533906 i & 0.8535533906 + 0.3535533906 i  \\\\\n"
" -0.3535533906 + 0.8535533906 i & 0.3535533906 + 0.1464466094 i  \\\\\n"
" \\end{bmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We can also initialize a starting quantum state vector and then evolve that state according to the sequence of operations described by the circuit.\n"
msgstr "Maaari rin tayong mag-initialize ng panimulang quantum state vector at pagkatapos ay i-evolve ang state na iyon ayon sa pagkakasunud-sunod ng mga operasyong inilarawan ng circuit.\n"

